            <ArticleTitle>Pain Management for Older African Americans in the Perianesthesia Setting: The &quot;Eight I's&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>181-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jopan.2015.01.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1089-9472(15)00072-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>National legislation (Affordable Care Act) emphasizes quality and equitable pain care for all patient populations, but frequently, pain management is not effective and equitable in African American (AA) elders, placing them at higher risk for severe pain and persistent pain. Research shows that AAs are less likely to receive guideline-based pain care. This underscores the need for perianesthesia nurses to be knowledgeable and capable of integrating cultural practices and evidence-based recommendations into their care of older AAs to ensure adequate pain management in this vulnerable population. This article describes differences and disparities in pain management in AA older adults and provides a cultural framework to guide perianesthesia pain management.</AbstractText>
